Xbox 360 project template (decompdecomp, stage 3)
=================================================

This is a deliberately small scaffold. It needs a user-supplied compiler: decompdecomp
bundles or downloads neither xsdk nor Microsoft's XDK. Nothing here is run when a
project is created; configure.py and compile.py are here for the user's first build.

Configure:
    python tools/configure.py VERSION
    ninja

Which compiler is used, in order:
    XSDK_CC           an explicit compiler path (native, no wine wrapper)
    xdk360 kit        a registered real Microsoft XDK (cl.exe), preferred when set up
    xsdk kit          a registered free xsdk GCC toolchain
    PATH              powerpc-xsdk-elf-gcc (the default)

Environment (all optional except that the compiler must exist):
    XSDK_CC                  compiler to call (see the order above)
    XSDK_FLAGS               flags passed to the compiler (defaults to the list below)
    DECOMPNET_WINE           the wine binary, when the real XDK is used
    DECOMPNET_WINE_PREFIX    its wine prefix (never your ~/.wine)

Real Xbox 360 XDK (xdk360 kit):
    The real Microsoft PowerPC cl.exe is the compiler retail games were built with.
    On Linux and macOS it is a Windows program and needs Wine, which you install and
    keep updated yourself; decompdecomp does not pin or ship a Windows runtime.
    configure.py wraps the chosen cl.exe with wine and compile.py then uses MSVC
    switches (/I, /c, /Fo), so XSDK_FLAGS must be set to MSVC-style flags too:
        XSDK_FLAGS='/O2 /I<your XDK include path>' python tools/configure.py VERSION
    The copy's own headers and libraries (XDK/include/xbox, XDK/lib/xbox) are needed
    for real builds; most compiler-only extractions do not include them. The
    compile-over-Wine path is wired up and the compilers are verified to answer /?
    under Wine, but byte-matching a real game with it is NOT verified here.

Default flags, chosen for PowerPC64 / Cell / the xsdk runtime (xsdk only):
    -mcpu=cell -fno-pic -mcall-xsdk -ffreestanding
    -fno-builtin -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti

These are starting flags, not verified for every game; check them against the
game's own build before trusting a match. tools/compile.py produces the ELF
objects that objdiff compares (the "base" side). The "target" objects are cut
from the XEX2 by a delinker that is not part of this stage, so configure.py
writes their conventional paths but does not build them. Ghidra-based function
discovery, for games without a functions.jsonl map, is a later option.
